Below is the modeling architecture I would recommend after reading the attached feature inventory, the parameter file, and the current spatial code.

The main conclusion is:

> You should **not** treat “target correction,” “spatial lag modeling,” and “final AVM learning” as one model. They should be three distinct layers with different allowed features, different leakage rules, and different validation logic.

---

# 1. What the available features are actually doing

The attached feature inventory contains far more than one homogeneous feature matrix. It contains **target variables, sale metadata, property characteristics, spatial identifiers, geographic coordinates, census/proximity features, prior assessment outputs, sale-validation information, and precomputed spatial lags**. These should not all be treated as ordinary predictors. 

The parameter file says the current CCAO-style model uses LightGBM with objective `"rmse"`, 10-fold CV, rolling-origin-style parameters, and a time split where the most recent 10% of pre-assessment sales are treated as test data. The current `model.predictor.all` contains 95 predictors and `model.predictor.categorical` contains 23 categorical predictors. 

The current spatial experiment code then wraps the repo preprocessing and fits a linear/ridge estimator using those same `params.yaml::model.predictor.all` features; spatial features are added on top of that full-X setting, and the TEST/ASSESS splits mirror the quick-test logic. 

---

## 1.1 Target and sale-time variables

These are:

```text
meta_sale_price
meta_sale_date
time_sale_year
time_sale_day
time_sale_quarter_of_year
time_sale_month_of_year
time_sale_day_of_year
time_sale_day_of_month
time_sale_day_of_week
time_sale_post_covid
```

`meta_sale_price` is the observed transaction price and should become:

[
y_i=\log(\text{sale price}_i).
]

`meta_sale_date` and the derived `time_sale_*` variables are **not ordinary property characteristics**. They represent the market date of the observed sale. In training, they describe when the sale happened. At assessment, they must be set consistently to the assessment valuation date, not to an unknown future sale date. The parameter file explicitly defines the 2025 assessment context and the statutory sale/prediction date as `2025-01-01`, with property data pulled from data year 2024. 

So these variables are valid only if interpreted as **market-date controls**.

Recommended use:

| Feature           | Use                                                                           |
| ----------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `meta_sale_price` | target only                                                                   |
| `meta_sale_date`  | target correction, temporal split, spatial cutoff                             |
| `time_sale_*`     | allowed in final AVM only if replaced by assessment-date values at deployment |

