# 2. Target modeling: how to correct sale price into a better market-value proxy

The correct conceptual model is:

[
\log S_i
========

## M_i(T)

\Delta_i(t_i,T)
+
\eta_i,
]

where:

* (S_i) is the observed sale price;
* (M_i(T)) is latent market value at assessment date (T);
* (\Delta_i(t_i,T)) is market movement between sale date (t_i) and assessment date (T);
* (\eta_i) is transaction noise, sale-specific deviation, or unobserved condition.

The only target correction I would do with high confidence is:

[
y_i^{adj}(T)
============

\log S_i
+
\widehat{\tau}(s_i,T)
---------------------

\widehat{\tau}(s_i,t_i).
]

That is, **time-adjust sale prices to the assessment date**, possibly with local geography/property-type variation.

This has strong support in appraisal and price-index logic. IAAO ratio-study guidance says market value cannot be directly observed, but can be represented by sale prices that have been confirmed, screened, and adjusted as necessary; it also says sales under normal circumstances are generally good indicators of market value.  Mass appraisal standards describe model specification and calibration as identifying value-related supply/demand/property factors and estimating their value contributions, and they explicitly recognize geographic stratification and comparable-sales approaches for residential valuation. 

---

## 2.1 Target correction model A: global time adjustment

Current code estimates a simple global time slope:

[
\log S_i = \alpha+\beta t_i+\varepsilon_i.
]

Then:

[
y_i^{adj}(T)=y_i+\widehat\beta(T-t_i).
]

This is useful as a baseline, but it is too simple for final work. It assumes the same appreciation rate across Cook County, across property classes, and across market segments.

Use it as:

> baseline target correction, not final correction.

The current code uses this global time slope to build `time_adjusted_price` and `scaled_time_adjusted_price` neighbor features. 

---

## 2.2 Target correction model B: hedonic time-dummy correction

Estimate:

[
\log S_i
========

h(X_i^{struct},G_i)
+
\gamma_{month(i)}
+
\varepsilon_i.
]

Then use only the time effect:

[
y_i^{adj}(T)
============

y_i+\widehat\gamma_T-\widehat\gamma_{t_i}.
]

Here (h(X_i^{struct},G_i)) controls for property mix so that time effects are not contaminated by changes in which properties happen to sell.

This is standard price-index logic: real estate price-index literature contrasts hedonic methods, which use covariates to control property heterogeneity, and repeat-sales methods, which use multiple transactions of the same property; hedonic methods treat observed prices as composites of structural and locational implicit prices. ([cowles.yale.edu][1])

Recommended controls for (h(\cdot)):

```text
char_class
meta_township_code
meta_nbhd_code
char_bldg_sf
char_land_sf
char_yrblt
char_rooms
char_beds
bath variables
garage/basement/attic/air/heat
lat/lon or x/y spatial smooth terms
```

Important: use property features only to estimate the time index. Do not subtract their effects from the target. The final AVM should still learn structural effects.

---

## 2.3 Target correction model C: local hedonic time correction

Estimate:

[
\log S_i
========

h(X_i)
+
\gamma_{g_i,t_i}
+
\varepsilon_i,
]

where (g_i) is a market area:

```text
triad
township
neighborhood
census tract
school district
spatial cluster
```

Then:

[
y_i^{adj}(T)
============

y_i+\widehat\gamma_{g_i,T}-\widehat\gamma_{g_i,t_i}.
]

This is better than a global time slope because local markets move differently.

But raw area-by-month effects can be sparse. So I would use shrinkage:

[
\gamma_{g,t}
============

\gamma_t+\delta_{g,t},
\qquad
\delta_{g,t}\sim \text{shrink toward }0.
]

In practice:

* global month effects;
* triad × quarter effects;
* township × year effects;
* neighborhood × year effects only if enough sales;
* fallback hierarchy when sparse.

This is probably the best label-correction direction.

---

## 2.4 Target correction model D: spatial-temporal surface

Estimate:

[
\log S_i
========

h(X_i)
+
\tau(s_i,t_i)
+
\varepsilon_i,
]

where (s_i=(x_i,y_i)).

Then:

[
y_i^{adj}(T)
============

y_i+\widehat\tau(s_i,T)-\widehat\tau(s_i,t_i).
]

Possible estimators:

* spatial splines;
* tensor-product spline in (x,y,t);
* local regression;
* Gaussian-process-like low-rank smoother;
* grid-cell time effects with shrinkage.

This is theoretically appealing, but more fragile than area-time effects. I would not start here as the main correction. I would use it after proving that township/neighborhood time effects are too coarse.

---

## 2.5 Target correction model E: repeat-sales validation

If a property sells multiple times, use:

[
\log S_{i,t_2}-\log S_{i,t_1}
=============================

\tau(t_2)-\tau(t_1)+\epsilon_i.
]

Repeat-sales methods are standard for house-price indexes, but they use only properties that transact multiple times and can suffer selection bias because repeat-sale properties may not represent the whole market. ([cowles.yale.edu][1])

For your project, I would use repeat sales mainly to **validate** the hedonic time correction, not as the primary correction.

---

## 2.6 What not to do for target correction

Do not build:

[
y_i^{proxy}
===========

\text{local average of neighbor sale prices}
]

and then train a model on that as the target. That turns the target into a smoothed prediction and can wash out real property-level variation.

Use spatial comparable information as a **feature** or **residual correction**, not as the main target, except for the time-index adjustment above.

