# 3. Spatial modeling: what makes sense here

The current spatial code is already close to the right family. It is not a simultaneous spatial-lag model. It is a **temporally valid comparable-sales spatial feature**.

The code supports these feature types:

```text
raw_price
time_adjusted_price
residual
scaled_price
scaled_time_adjusted_price
```

and computes spatial-time scores:

[
\text{score}_{ij}
=================

\frac{d_{ij}^{space}}{h_s}
+
\frac{d_{ij}^{time}}{h_t}.
]

Then it uses exponential weights:

[
w_{ij}=\exp(-\text{score}_{ij}).
]

The current implementation also uses KDTree candidate caches and expands candidate pools adaptively before applying temporal and class filters. 

This is good.

---

## 3.1 Best spatial feature: scaled time-adjusted comparable value

The strongest feature is:

[
z_i^{scaled-time}
=================

\frac{\sum_{j\in N(i)} w_{ij}
\left[
y_j
+
\widehat\beta(t_i-t_j)
+
\hat f_0(X_i)
-------------

\hat f_0(X_j)
\right]}
{\sum_{j\in N(i)}w_{ij}}.
]

This says:

> Take a nearby past sale, move it to the target date, then adjust it from the neighbor’s baseline structural value to the target property’s baseline structural value.

That is exactly the right comparable-sales logic.

It is much better than raw spatial average because raw average confounds:

* location value;
* structure quality;
* size;
* date;
* sale-specific noise.

Your results already show this feature family is one of the best prediction-oriented candidates. For example, the attached logs show `assess_full_X_plus_scaled_time_adjusted_price_K20_hs0.75_ht500.0` achieves strong assess performance with `r2_log=0.8171`, `rmse_log=0.2996`, `r2_price=0.8553`, and `rmse_price=146077`. 

This should be the primary spatial feature family.

---

## 3.2 Residual spatial feature

Define:

[
r_j=y_j^{adj}-\hat f_0(X_j).
]

Then:

[
z_i^{resid}
===========

\frac{\sum_{j\in N(i)}w_{ij}r_j}{\sum_{j\in N(i)}w_{ij}}.
]

Prediction:

[
\hat y_i=\hat f_0(X_i)+g(z_i^{resid}).
]

This captures local market effects not explained by full (X).

This is conceptually similar to a spatial-error correction:

[
y=X\beta+u,\qquad u=\lambda Wu+\epsilon,
]

but implemented in a deployment-safe way because it uses only prior sales.

This should be your second main spatial family.

However, residual features are more sensitive to:

* base-model misspecification;
* temporal drift;
* cross-fitting quality;
* sale noise.

So they must be computed using cross-fitted residuals on training data. Otherwise, training-side residuals are in-sample and too optimistic.

---

## 3.3 Raw and time-adjusted price lags

Raw neighbor average:

[
z_i^{raw}
=========

\frac{\sum_j w_{ij}y_j}{\sum_jw_{ij}}.
]

Time-adjusted neighbor average:

[
z_i^{time}
==========

\frac{\sum_j w_{ij}(y_j+\widehat\beta(t_i-t_j))}{\sum_jw_{ij}}.
]

These are useful benchmarks but not final candidates.

Your results suggest time-adjusted price lags have weaker predictive accuracy but less negative regressivity slope than residual/scaled features. That makes them useful for fairness/diagnostic comparison, not necessarily for final prediction. 

---

## 3.4 Classical spatial lag, spatial error, GWR, and MGWR

The literature gives several alternatives:

| Method            | Form                                        | Role here                            |
| ----------------- | ------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------ |
| Spatial lag       | (y=\rho Wy+X\beta+\epsilon)                 | diagnostic / benchmark               |
| Spatial error     | (y=X\beta+u,\ u=\lambda Wu+\epsilon)        | residual correction analogy          |
| GWR               | local coefficients by location              | diagnostic for spatial heterogeneity |
| MGWR              | covariates vary at different spatial scales | diagnostic / research benchmark      |
| Kriging residuals | smooth spatial residual process             | residual correction benchmark        |

Spatial models are widely discussed in mass appraisal because location creates spatial heterogeneity and spatial autocorrelation; GWR, spatial lag, and spatial error models are common alternatives in real estate valuation studies. ([ODU Digital Commons][2])

But for your operational AVM, I would not make a simultaneous spatial lag model the primary approach. The problem is deployment:

[
Wy_i
]

requires neighboring outcomes. For unsold assessment properties, those outcomes are unavailable unless you construct (W) only from historical sales. That is exactly why your current comparable-sales lag is more appropriate.

