# 5. The best overall modeling architecture

I would structure the project as five candidate pipelines.

---

## Pipeline A: raw target baseline

[
y_i=\log S_i.
]

Train:

[
\hat y_i=f(X_i).
]

Use current LightGBM configuration.

Purpose:

> baseline against which all target/spatial corrections must improve.

---

## Pipeline B: time-adjusted target

Estimate a fold-specific local time index:

[
\widehat\tau(g,t).
]

Construct:

[
y_i^{adj}(T)
============

\log S_i+\widehat\tau(g_i,T)-\widehat\tau(g_i,t_i).
]

Train:

[
\hat y_i=f(X_i).
]

Best version:

```text
hedonic time-dummy model
with class/property controls
and township/triad/neighborhood time effects with shrinkage
```

Purpose:

> test whether correcting sale prices to assessment-date market conditions reduces bias and improves stability.

---

## Pipeline C: robust target learning

Do not change (y). Change the loss.

Options:

```text
MSE / RMSE
MAE / median regression
Huber loss
quantile regression τ = 0.5
Student-t likelihood
```

Median/quantile regression is especially relevant because quantile regression generalizes least-squares conditional-mean modeling to conditional quantile functions; median regression minimizes absolute error. ([American Economic Association][3]) More generally, point forecasting theory says the loss must match the target functional: squared error targets mean-like functionals, while quantile losses target quantiles. ([arXiv][4])

Purpose:

> test whether raw sale prices are too noisy for MSE.

---

## Pipeline D: full X + spatial comparable feature

Train a base model:

[
\hat f_0(X).
]

Construct:

[
z_i^{scaled-time}
=================

\frac{\sum_j w_{ij}
[
y_j+\widehat\tau(t_i,t_j)+\hat f_0(X_i)-\hat f_0(X_j)
]}
{\sum_jw_{ij}}.
]

Train final model:

[
\hat y_i=f_1(X_i,z_i^{scaled-time},d_i,n_i,a_i),
]

where:

```text
d_i = mean neighbor distance
n_i = number of neighbors
a_i = mean neighbor age
fallback_i = whether class fallback was used
```

Purpose:

> exploit comparable-sales information without replacing the whole AVM.

This is currently the most promising spatial direction.

---

## Pipeline E: full X + residual spatial correction

Train base model:

[
\hat f_0(X).
]

Cross-fit residuals:

[
r_j=y_j-\hat f_0^{(-fold)}(X_j).
]

Construct:

[
z_i^{resid}
===========

\frac{\sum_jw_{ij}r_j}{\sum_jw_{ij}}.
]

Prediction:

[
\hat y_i^{final}
================

\hat f_0(X_i)+g(z_i^{resid}).
]

Purpose:

> capture local under/overprediction patterns not explained by the AVM.

This is conceptually strong but must be cross-fitted.

