# 9. Adversarial review: what could go wrong

## Problem 1: target correction can hide bias

If the time/geography correction is too flexible, it may absorb the very price-related inequity you are trying to measure.

Fix:

```text
estimate correction on training folds only
compare raw vs corrected labels
report correction size by price decile
report correction size by geography
```

---

## Problem 2: spatial features can leak outcomes

Any spatial lag built from all sales can leak future sale prices.

Fix:

```text
strict prior-sale cutoff
no self-comps
fold-specific construction
separate train/eval history rules
```

The current code correctly emphasizes prior sales and candidate history, but you must confirm `allow_sequential_eval_history` is set consistently with the evaluation question. 

---

## Problem 3: group filtering may still be active

You previously wanted to remove group filtering. The current attached code still has a default group filter column:

```text
DEFAULT_GROUP_FILTER_COL = "meta_modeling_group"
```

and the pool index uses `group_filter_col` if present. 

If the goal is truly “no group filter,” change the default to `None` or pass a non-existing/missing group filter and confirm the candidate pool is universal except for class logic.

---

## Problem 4: ACS variables can improve market prediction but complicate fairness

ACS variables may improve market-value prediction because they proxy neighborhood demand. But they can also encode socioeconomic patterns. Removing them may reduce accuracy and potentially worsen residual inequity; keeping them may raise policy concerns.

Fix:

```text
run with ACS
run without ACS
run with ACS only in calibration/audit
compare decile curves and triad/township residuals
```

---

## Problem 5: prior assessments can make the model look good while preserving inequity

Prior assessed values and tax bills may improve predictive fit but are not clean market value signals.

Fix:

```text
exclude from main model
include only as separate benchmark
```

